s just  looking at the manifesto for the last  election  and part of that was to say that  the energy and mail and rail would be uh  nationalized  okay of course  every party changes their manifestos  over time i've just been talking about  the immediate measures that labour would  be taking right now to deal with those  energy costs i think that's what many  people are worried about having heard  the news recently about the hike in the  cap that's coming up very very soon but  you know as labour has been very clear  in seeing we are pragmatic we're  pragmatic about how for example our  energy system is run in our country  we're pragmatic about the role of public  ownership within energy it needs to  operate within those fiscal rules that  were set out by rachel reeves at our  last party conference needs to work for  our country and that's the key thing  that's of importance for us as labour we  want to get a grip on those long-term  problems that have affected our country  i would say when it comes to energy as  well in particular we want to get a grip  on the extremely poor quality of energy  insulation that we have in our country  we've got some of the worst insulated  homes in all of europe right now that's  why we said that we would be insulating  britain as well so again that big  contrast between labour that's pragmatic  that's clear about what we would change  and the conservatives when there's  simply no plan at all  well you say that there's a thought that  potentially vat could be cut  and i know that was something that  gordon brown actually implemented didn't  he when he was prime minister is cutting  v80 a viable option to save the public  from higher bills  well we think it's important to focus  specifically on energy because not only  is energy critical for bills as you said  okay it's also critical of course when  it comes to inflation it contributes a  lot to that spiraling inflation that  we're seeing at the moment that's why  labour's plan is firmly focused on  energy bills  on stopping those bills from increasing  again with that projected rise in the  cap we set out exactly how we would  deliver that now we've seen a whole  range of different policy kites being  flown by the different conservative  candidates neither of them have  explained how they will pay for what  they're setting out neither of them have  actually stuck over time to what they  said they would put into practice a big  difference there compared to labour  because we've been saying since january  that there needed to be a proper  watertight windfall tax on the oil and  gas companies they've been making record  profits we should be using that money to  get people's bills down that's what  labour would be doing right now  um there's vat on energy bills isn't  there  there is an image built down in flat key  we call for that v80 to be removed we  called for that to be removed a very  long time ago it seems as i said that  some of the candidates now may be  supporting that some of the conservative  continuity candidates trust uh and rishi  sunak do seem to be saying now that they  might support that of course labour  again at the time set out how we would  be paying for that we're not seeing that  from the conservative candidates that is  a problem but we're particularly not  seeing k that action targeted on energy  bills under labour we would not see that  cap increasing uh in the coming months  because we would be using that windfall  tax to get those bills down neither of  the conservative candidates have  promised to do that but they should be  saying they would do it now because that  would take so much worry off so many  people's shoulders  okay um it's always great to see ms  dodds thanks so much for taking the time  pleasure
 

